# The Myth of the Two Rivers

In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite sides of a barren valley. Each village possessed a sacred spring that produced five measures of water daily.

One day, a wandering sage told them: "If you pour your water into the valley, it will flow to the other village and triple in abundance. The receiving village may then choose what to return."

The first village, led by Elder Ayana, poured three measures into the valley. The water multiplied to nine and reached the second village. Elder Kofi, grateful, returned six measures. Both villages prospered, their gardens blooming.

But doubt crept in like morning frost. "What if they keep everything?" whispered some. "Why give when we could hoard?" muttered others.

Some days the waters flowed generously, creating abundance. Other days, fear won, and trickles or nothing passed between them. Each village watched, learned, and adjusted—building trust or withdrawing in caution.

The sage returned years later. "What have you learned?" she asked.

The elders looked at the valley—sometimes lush, sometimes barren. "That abundance requires faith," said Ayana. "And faith," added Kofi, "requires someone to believe first."

The waters still flow, carrying each village's answer.
